<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Muisca Confederation</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1540 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A union of chiefdoms in Colombia's eastern highlands whose gold offerings in Lake Guatavita gave rise to the legend of El Dorado.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Muisca settled the high plateau of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-and-independent-state-of-cundinamarca">Cundinamarca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boyaca-department">Boyacá</a>, in the Eastern Ranges of the Colombian <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/andecavi">Andes</a>, and by around 800 their chiefdoms had taken shape. Over the following centuries they grouped into larger confederations led by the zipa in the south and the zaque in the north.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Muisca society was divided between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zipazgo-of-bacata">Zipazgo of Bacatá</a> in the south, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zacazgo-of-hunza">Zacazgo of Hunza</a> in the north, and independent territories such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/iraca">Iraca</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tundama">Tundama</a>. Rulers inherited power through the maternal line, from uncle to nephew. The Muisca traded salt from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zipaquira">Zipaquirá</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nemocon">Nemocón</a>, emeralds and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth for gold, which they cast into tunjos, votive figures. The installation of a new ruler at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-guatavita">Lake Guatavita</a>, covered in gold dust, became the legend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-dorado">El Dorado</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-jimenez-de-quesada">Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada</a> led a Spanish expedition into Muisca lands in 1537, defeating the zipa and zaque within two years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Santa Fe de Bogotá</a> was founded in 1538 as the centre of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-kingdom-of-granada">New Kingdom of Granada</a>. The Muisca language died out in the 18th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/muisca-confederation.jpg" alt="Muisca Confederation" width="320" height="325"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapa_del_Territorio_Muisca.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1540 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tisquesusa, Quemuenchatocha, Sagipa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Muisca religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Muisca (Chibcha)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Muisca, Chibcha Confederation</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1170&amp;focus=muisca-confederation" aria-label="Muisca Confederation on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2622 2001 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Muisca Confederation lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2942 2257L4910 989M2942 2257L2945 2261M2942 2257L2967 2232"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0M2945 2261h0M2967 2232h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2942 2257h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2942 2257h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1170&amp;focus=muisca-confederation">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
